 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Credit cards are about 17 percentage points above the cash rate. I couldn't get personal loan figures.**\n\n- **Cash rate target:** 4.36% (monthly average for September 2026, the latest figure). It was 4.35% in January 2024 and was cut to 3.60% by late 2025. It rose back to 4.35% between February and June 2026.\n- **Standard credit card rate:** 21.24% per annum (September 2026). That is the advertised indicator rate from RBA table F5. The spread over the cash rate is about 16.9 points, up from about 15.7 points in January 2024 (card rate 20.07%, cash rate 4.35%).\n- **Personal loans (fixed, unsecured):** The RBA lending rate series returned no data for this product in either of two requests, so I can't give a current figure or compare it with the cash rate.\n\nThe card rate rose 0.25 points in September 2026, while the cash rate barely moved (+0.01 points). Card rates have been much less responsive to cash rate changes than the cash rate itself: they stayed at 20.99% from early 2025 until the September 2026 increase. The RBA notes that rates actually paid on new loans are lower than these advertised rates, which are in table F6.",
